1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,240
Tonight, at nine forty-one
p.m., the Moon is gone.

2
00:00:04,330 --> 00:00:05,683
No explosion.

3
00:00:05,773 --> 00:00:06,920
No flash.

4
00:00:07,010 --> 00:00:09,335
One moment it hangs over the city.

5
00:00:09,425 --> 00:00:11,569
The next, there is only black sky.

6
00:00:12,359 --> 00:00:13,821
So what breaks first?

7
00:00:13,911 --> 00:00:16,296
The answer is stranger than the movies.

8
00:00:16,386 --> 00:00:19,645
Because the most dangerous
change is the slowest one.

9
00:00:20,435 --> 00:00:21,724
First, the rules.

10
00:00:21,814 --> 00:00:25,201
Nothing in physics lets a
whole world simply vanish.

11
00:00:25,291 --> 00:00:27,773
Mass and energy don't just disappear.

12
00:00:27,863 --> 00:00:29,808
So this is a thought experiment.

13
00:00:30,598 --> 00:00:33,369
We delete the Moon
instantly and completely.

14
00:00:33,459 --> 00:00:35,824
Its light, its gravity, everything.

15
00:00:35,914 --> 00:00:40,698
Then we follow the consequences, from the
first second to the next hundred million

16
00:00:40,718 --> 00:00:41,319
years.

17
00:00:42,109 --> 00:00:45,813
And we'll keep score honestly:
what scientists have measured,

18
00:00:45,833 --> 00:00:50,051
what physics lets us infer, and
where the experts still disagree.

19
00:00:50,841 --> 00:00:52,037
Second zero.

20
00:00:52,127 --> 00:00:55,540
In a disaster movie, Earth
would be flung out of its orbit.

21
00:00:55,630 --> 00:00:56,946
That doesn't happen.

22
00:00:57,736 --> 00:01:01,343
Earth and the Moon actually
circle a shared balance point.

23
00:01:01,433 --> 00:01:04,279
Because Earth is about
eighty-one times heavier,

24
00:01:04,299 --> 00:01:09,117
that point sits inside our planet, roughly
four thousand seven hundred kilometers

25
00:01:09,137 --> 00:01:10,342
from the center.

26
00:01:11,132 --> 00:01:16,088
Every month, Earth makes a small loop around
it, at about twelve meters per second.

27
00:01:16,178 --> 00:01:20,216
Our speed around the Sun is nearly
thirty kilometers per second.

28
00:01:21,006 --> 00:01:24,253
Delete the Moon, and that little
loop simply straightens out.

29
00:01:24,343 --> 00:01:26,970
Our path around the Sun barely changes.

30
00:01:27,060 --> 00:01:28,715
Nobody would feel a thing.

31
00:01:29,505 --> 00:01:31,982
What people would notice
first is the dark.

32
00:01:32,772 --> 00:01:37,205
A full Moon on a clear night lights the
ground with about a quarter of a lux.

33
00:01:37,295 --> 00:01:40,836
Without it, a natural sky offers
starlight and faint airglow,

34
00:01:40,856 --> 00:01:42,682
around a hundredth of that.

35
00:01:43,472 --> 00:01:46,147
In cities, streetlights
would hide the change.

36
00:01:46,237 --> 00:01:48,841
But in the deserts, the
oceans and the forests,

37
00:01:48,861 --> 00:01:51,456
every night would become a new-moon night.

38
00:01:51,546 --> 00:01:52,429
Forever.

39
00:01:53,219 --> 00:01:55,354
Some animals are tuned to that light.

40
00:01:55,444 --> 00:01:58,667
Dung beetles in Africa steer
by polarized moonlight,

41
00:01:58,687 --> 00:02:03,615
and on moonless nights they hold their
course by the glow of the Milky Way.

42
00:02:04,405 --> 00:02:09,133
On coral reefs, many species spawn
together, timed to the lunar cycle.

43
00:02:09,223 --> 00:02:13,964
A light-sensitive gene in these corals
is most active under full-moon light.

44
00:02:14,754 --> 00:02:17,656
Without that clock, spawning
could drift out of sync,

45
00:02:17,676 --> 00:02:20,848
and eggs released on different
nights may never meet.

46
00:02:20,938 --> 00:02:24,241
How badly this would play out
is still an open question.

47
00:02:25,031 --> 00:02:26,128
Hour six.

48
00:02:26,218 --> 00:02:29,404
Along the coast, the tide
comes in, and stops short.

49
00:02:30,194 --> 00:02:32,532
The Moon raises most of our tides.

50
00:02:32,622 --> 00:02:37,874
The Sun raises tides too, but its tidal
pull is only about forty-six percent of the

51
00:02:37,894 --> 00:02:38,589
Moon's.

52
00:02:39,379 --> 00:02:41,125
So the tides don't vanish.

53
00:02:41,215 --> 00:02:44,475
They shrink, to roughly a
third of their typical size.

54
00:02:44,565 --> 00:02:49,519
And they follow the Sun's rhythm, with high
water at nearly the same time every day.

55
00:02:50,309 --> 00:02:52,242
There is no giant wave either.

56
00:02:52,332 --> 00:02:56,965
The ocean's tidal bulge is subtle,
well under a meter in open water.

57
00:02:57,055 --> 00:03:01,276
So the sea would settle over hours,
like an ordinary falling tide.

58
00:03:02,066 --> 00:03:04,587
Spring tides and neap tides end, too.

59
00:03:04,677 --> 00:03:09,814
They come from the Sun and Moon lining up,
and now there is nothing to line up with.

60
00:03:10,604 --> 00:03:11,691
That matters.

61
00:03:11,781 --> 00:03:16,531
Tides flush estuaries and feed the strip
of shore that is underwater half the day

62
00:03:16,551 --> 00:03:18,156
and dry the other half.

63
00:03:18,246 --> 00:03:23,327
With weaker tides, that strip narrows,
and the life adapted to it loses ground.

64
00:03:24,117 --> 00:03:25,068
Year one.

65
00:03:25,158 --> 00:03:27,885
There will never be another
total solar eclipse.

66
00:03:27,975 --> 00:03:30,071
Only the Moon could cover the Sun.

67
00:03:30,861 --> 00:03:32,962
Something quieter changes, too.

68
00:03:33,052 --> 00:03:36,557
Today, tidal friction acts
like a brake on Earth's spin.

69
00:03:36,647 --> 00:03:39,969
It adds roughly two milliseconds
to the day every century,

70
00:03:39,989 --> 00:03:44,790
and pushes the Moon about three point
eight centimeters farther away each year.

71
00:03:45,580 --> 00:03:48,502
Without the Moon, most of
that brake is released.

72
00:03:48,592 --> 00:03:50,874
You would never notice it in a lifetime.

73
00:03:50,964 --> 00:03:55,134
But over hundreds of millions of years,
Earth's days would stay shorter than they

74
00:03:55,154 --> 00:03:56,490
otherwise would have.

75
00:03:57,280 --> 00:03:58,986
Now, the real danger.

76
00:03:59,076 --> 00:04:00,287
And it is slow.

77
00:04:01,077 --> 00:04:04,763
Earth spins on a tilted axis,
about twenty-three degrees.

78
00:04:04,853 --> 00:04:07,006
That tilt is why we have seasons.

79
00:04:07,796 --> 00:04:12,735
Today it only rocks gently, between roughly
twenty-two and twenty-four and a half

80
00:04:12,755 --> 00:04:15,515
degrees, over tens of thousands of years.

81
00:04:16,305 --> 00:04:18,290
The Moon is a big reason why.

82
00:04:18,380 --> 00:04:23,507
Its gravity tugs on Earth's bulging waist,
and drives about two-thirds of the slow

83
00:04:23,527 --> 00:04:26,162
wobble of our axis, called precession.

84
00:04:26,952 --> 00:04:30,210
That wobble keeps Earth's spin
out of step with the gentle,

85
00:04:30,230 --> 00:04:32,480
repeating tugs of the other planets.

86
00:04:33,270 --> 00:04:38,165
Remove the Moon, and the wobble slows down,
and it can fall into rhythm with those

87
00:04:38,185 --> 00:04:38,735
tugs.

88
00:04:38,825 --> 00:04:44,561
In 1993, Jacques Laskar and his colleagues
calculated that a moonless Earth's tilt

89
00:04:44,581 --> 00:04:50,377
could wander chaotically, anywhere from
near zero to about eighty-five degrees.

90
00:04:51,167 --> 00:04:55,606
Near the top of that range, much of
the planet would face months of endless

91
00:04:55,626 --> 00:04:58,314
daylight, followed by months of darkness.

92
00:04:59,104 --> 00:05:00,623
But here is the twist.

93
00:05:00,713 --> 00:05:05,521
In 2011, a team led by Jack
Lissauer ran direct simulations,

94
00:05:05,541 --> 00:05:08,205
and found far narrower swings.

95
00:05:08,295 --> 00:05:12,364
Roughly ten to twenty degrees,
over hundreds of millions of years.

96
00:05:13,154 --> 00:05:15,018
Even that is a different world.

97
00:05:15,108 --> 00:05:18,131
Today the tilt shifts by
about two and a half degrees.

98
00:05:18,221 --> 00:05:21,588
A twenty-degree swing would
redraw where ice sheets grow,

99
00:05:21,608 --> 00:05:24,769
where monsoons fall, and
where crops can survive.

100
00:05:25,559 --> 00:05:27,267
And none of it happens fast.

101
00:05:27,357 --> 00:05:32,026
These changes unfold over tens of
thousands to millions of years.

102
00:05:32,116 --> 00:05:33,293
Slow for us.

103
00:05:33,383 --> 00:05:34,746
Fast for a planet.

104
00:05:35,536 --> 00:05:37,006
So, what breaks first?

105
00:05:37,096 --> 00:05:38,322
Not the orbit.

106
00:05:38,412 --> 00:05:39,992
Not the length of the day.

107
00:05:40,082 --> 00:05:41,338
First, the dark.

108
00:05:41,428 --> 00:05:42,685
Then the tides.

109
00:05:42,775 --> 00:05:46,060
And last, slowly, the steady
footing of our seasons.

110
00:05:46,850 --> 00:05:49,886
In reality, the Moon
isn't going anywhere soon.

111
00:05:49,976 --> 00:05:55,060
Laser beams bounced off mirrors left by
Apollo astronauts show it drifting away by

112
00:05:55,080 --> 00:05:57,944
just three point eight centimeters a year.

113
00:05:58,734 --> 00:06:00,739
Our scenario is impossible.

114
00:06:00,829 --> 00:06:02,897
But it shows what the Moon really is.

115
00:06:02,987 --> 00:06:03,895
A lamp.

116
00:06:03,985 --> 00:06:05,639
A clock for the oceans.

117
00:06:05,729 --> 00:06:08,476
And a hand on the steering
wheel of our seasons.

118
00:06:09,266 --> 00:06:10,516
Tonight, look up.

119
00:06:10,606 --> 00:06:13,134
It is still there,
holding everything steady.
